From 4bec79c47d1342db6a5aece5b08270cea5d6f3ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 15:11:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 16/37] vsock: separate TSI from the guest-IPC half of the
 device

virtio-vsock in this tree carries two unrelated things. TSI hijacks the guest's
AF_INET/AF_UNIX sockets and re-issues them on the host; guest IPC is the plain
`krun_add_vsock_port2()` path, where a guest port is bridged to a host endpoint.
Only TSI is Unix-socket based end to end, but the whole module was gated to Unix
as one unit, which swept up the IPC path with it.

Draw the line where it actually falls:

- `tsi_stream.rs` and `tsi_dgram.rs` become Unix-only modules. They hold 25 of
  the 50 errors the module produces when built for Windows, and none of them is
  worth porting: Boks displaces TSI with virtio-net.
- The DGRAM dispatch in `send_dgram_pkt()` is TSI's control plane in its
  entirety -- every port constant is a TSI opcode, and every DGRAM data packet
  is addressed to a proxy only that control plane can create -- so the whole
  match is gated rather than each arm.
- `Proxy`'s `getpeername`/`sendto_addr`/`sendto_data`/`listen`/`accept` are
  reachable only from those arms; the guest-IPC proxies answer all five with
  `todo!()`/`unreachable!()`. They leave the trait on non-Unix.
- The socket-address payloads in `packet.rs` (`SockaddrStorage`, `parse_address`
  and the readers that use them) exist only to carry TSI addresses, and take
  `nix` with them.

What remains is written against whichever `RawFd`/`AsRawFd` pair the target
provides -- `utils::windows::RawFd` is a `HANDLE`, and the muxer only ever
stores these values and passes them to `Epoll::ctl` -- so the muxer, the muxer
thread, the RX queue, the event handler and the device itself stop being
Unix-specific. `ProxyUpdate::new_proxy` becomes a named `AcceptedProxy` alias
because its accepted-endpoint type genuinely differs per platform; the Unix form
keeps the `AddressFamily` that TSI's reverse path needs, and nothing else does.

`TsiConnectReq::for_ipc()` replaces the placeholder the muxer used to build
inline when driving a guest-IPC connect through the shared `Proxy::connect()`.

No behaviour change on Unix: this commit only moves `#[cfg]` boundaries.

